T6 have more remaining, so maybe better to discard B7 or T8.\n\nBetween B7 and T8, which is better? If I discard B7, I can form a Dots chow with B3-4-5. If I discard T8, form a Bamboos chow with T4-5-6. Both are similar. But the Dots chow might be easier since B5 is available. Also, I have B3 and B4, which are consecutive, so forming a chow with B5 is straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard T8, I have T4 and T5. Getting T6 would complete the chow. But I also have T1 and T2, which might not be useful unless I get T3 (which is out). So maybe the Dots chow is more viable.\n\nTherefore, discarding B7 seems like a good move. It allows me to form a Dots chow with B5, and possibly other chows in other suits if the right tiles are drawn.\n\nAnother consideration: the current hand has a lot of single tiles. Discarding a single tile (B7) doesn't remove any potential for pairs. If I discard a tile that's part of a pair (like W3 or W5), that would be worse.\n\nSo the best options are to discard B7, T8, or W6. Among these, B7 and T8 are better because they don't affect existing pairs.\n\nBetween B7 and T8, I'll go with B7. Because forming a Dots chow is more straightforward, and B5 is available in higher quantity. Also, the Dots suit might be less contested since the other players have melds in Characters and Bamboos. The player across has a Characters chow, so they might be focusing on that suit, leaving Dots less contested.\n\nSo the final decision is to discard B7.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The best move is to discard B7 to form a potential chow in the Dots suit if B5 is drawn. This maintains the concealed hand and pairs in Characters, maximizing chances for a high-scoring hand.\nReason: Discarding B7 allows forming a Dots chow (B3-B4-B5) with available B5 tiles (4 left). Keeping W3 and W5 pairs supports Seven Pairs or All Pairs potential. This move avoids breaking existing pairs and focuses on viable sequences.\nAnswer: Play B7"}
